        <p>The 30-hour week keeps $75,775.06 after federal tax alone, which is $14,182.56 more than the $75,000 salary keeps, on ten fewer hours a week. It also keeps $48.57 for every hour worked, against $47.11 for a full-time hour at the same rate, because less of a 30-hour year’s $79,060.00 of taxable income reaches the 22% bracket. The same $75,775.06 shows up in the unpaid-weeks table as the thirteen-unpaid-weeks year, because thirteen unpaid weeks leave the same 1,560 paid hours. The trade is real, though: the 30-hour week gives up $22,213.42 of take-home against the 40-hour week, or $1,851.12 a month, and it usually gives up benefits too, since 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start. The point is narrower than that: at $61, part-time is not a pay cut to the bone. It still clears $75,000 of take-home and still beats a $75,000 salary.</p>
