      <section class="article-section" id="good-salary">
        <h2>Does $61 Count as Good Pay?</h2>
        <p>Against national benchmarks, $61 clears the bar comfortably. The median hourly wage is roughly $24.51 an hour, which annualizes to $50,980.80. So $61 sits $36.49 an hour, or 148.9%, above the median, worth $75,899.20 a year more than the median worker’s full-time gross. It also sits well above the 75th-percentile wage of $38.71 an hour (BLS, May 2025), which puts $61 in the top quarter of all U.S. wages, and it is 8.41 times the $7.25 federal minimum wage. Median full-time earnings tell the same story another way: at $1,251 a week (BLS, second quarter of 2026) the median full-time worker earns $65,052.00 a year, and $126,880.00 is 95.0% above that. By the usual “above median” test, $61 is good pay.</p>
        <p>The test that matters more is the monthly one. Federal-only take-home is $8,165.71 a month, and the common 30% rent rule puts the rent budget at $2,449.71 a month on that figure. That is comfortable in most mid-size cities and tight in the most expensive ones, and it is the reason the same wage feels different in different places. Run the 50/30/20 budget on that $8,165.71 and it splits into $4,082.85 for needs, $2,449.71 for wants, and $1,633.14 for savings and extra debt payments. That is a workable split, as long as the needs column is honest about state tax and health premiums. Two cautions before you decide. The $8,165.71 assumes a full 40-hour week all year with no unpaid weeks and no state tax, and it assumes no 401(k) or health premium comes off first. A 5% 401(k) contribution is $6,344.00 a year, which costs $4,821.44 of take-home after the $1,522.56 federal saving, or $401.79 a month and $185.44 a biweekly check, as our <a href="/articles/how-does-a-401k-contribution-affect-your-paycheck/">401(k)-paycheck guide</a> prices in dollars. One milestone to keep in sight: $10,000 of spending money takes 212.27 take-home hours, or 5.31 weeks, not the 163.93 gross hours the rate alone suggests. Our <a href="/articles/how-much-is-60-an-hour-a-year/">$60-an-hour guide</a> and <a href="/articles/how-much-is-59-an-hour-a-year/">$59-an-hour guide</a> put the same benchmarks at their rates, so you can see where $61 sits on the whole ladder.</p>
      </section>
