        <p>Start with the monthly row, because it fools people first. It divides by 12, not by 4. Four weeks is only $9,760.00, and the $813.33 gap is the extra 1.33 weeks the average month really holds. Then the biweekly row. Biweekly and semi-monthly are not the same check: biweekly is 26 paychecks of $4,880.00, semi-monthly is 24 paychecks of $5,286.67, and the two extra biweekly checks a year are what make the totals equal. A month with three biweekly checks pays $14,640.00 gross and $11,306.37 of take-home. A normal two-check month pays $9,760.00 and $7,537.58. That swing is why biweekly pay feels lumpy. Our <a href="/articles/how-many-paychecks-in-a-year/">paychecks-per-year guide</a> maps the three-paycheck months for every 2026 cycle, and our <a href="/articles/biweekly-vs-semi-monthly-pay-difference/">biweekly-versus-semimonthly comparison</a> runs the two schedules through a full year on the same salary.</p>
