        <p>Read the last column top to bottom, because the pattern is the point of this section: the fewer hours you work, the more each hour keeps. That is $51.11 at 20 hours a week against $47.11 at 40, a $4.00-an-hour gap on the same $61.00 rate. The reason is the bracket. None of a 20-hour year’s $47,340.00 of taxable income reaches the 22% bracket, while a 25-hour year’s $63,200.00 of taxable income runs $12,800.00 into it, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket shows up at all. Three things in that table matter more than the headline. First, the 35-hour week crosses $100,000 gross on its own, at $111,020.00, and its $86,932.57 of take-home already beats a $110,000 salary by $717.57, before overtime or a raise. Second, the 37.5-hour paid week, common in offices that pay for a 7.5-hour day, is $118,950.00 gross, which still beats a $110,000 salary by $8,950.00 gross and $6,296.32 of take-home. A 2.5-hour shorter week costs $7,930.00 gross and $5,477.16 of take-home. Third, the step from 30 to 40 hours is worth $31,720.00 gross and $22,213.42 of take-home, or $1,851.12 a month. That is the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Two more results are worth knowing. A 25-hour week at $61 beats a $75,000 salary’s take-home by $3,025.05, so even a part-time schedule at this rate out-earns a solid full-time salary. Part-time keeps a bigger share: at 20 hours you keep 83.8% of gross, against 77.2% full-time, and a 25-hour week at $61 beats a $70,000 salary’s take-home by $6,542.55. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
